WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.100
A recent Knit it Now Machine-Knitable

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:10.300
was this beautiful wrap that was knit
diagonally. Using self-striping yarn

3
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.100
the designer started at a point

4
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:19.300
knit to the width of the wrap and then
continued knitting diagonally.

5
00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.580
She added a mixture of Garter stitch
sections with some open work sections,

6
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.980
and this added texture
and interest to the wrap.

7
00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.940
The construction is pretty simple.

8
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:36.380
It looks like this. Cast on three stitches.

9
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.780
Increase one stitch at the beginning

10
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:43.340
of every row until the piece
is as wide as you like.

11
00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.520
Then decrease on one side and increase

12
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:52.340
on the opposite side,
forming that diagonal shaping.

13
00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.900
To finish, decrease one stitch

14
00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:02.480
at the beginning of every row until there
are three stitches in work and bind off.

15
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.940
As machine knitters,

16
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:10.050
we can easily use this technique and her
design as inspiration for our knitting.

17
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.440
But there are some things to consider.

18
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:20.040
Most often, hand-knit diagonals
are knit with Garter stitch.

19
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.740
By its nature, the gauge of Garter
stitch is more square than Stockinette.

20
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:33.060
Compare these samples and you'll see
that the point of my hand knit diagonal

21
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:38.900
swatch is more of a square shape.
Where the point of my stockinette

22
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:44.570
machine-knit sample is
somewhat distorted by comparison.

23
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.560
Just something to consider.

24
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.570
Another thing is the fact
that stockinette curls.

25
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.440
There are a number of ways to reduce

26
00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:02.100
the curl, and for this inspiration piece,
machine knitters could alternate some rows

27
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:06.850
of reverse Stockinette,
alternating the knit and purl sides.

28
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:13.660
We could manually add some Garter ridges
or add stripes of another stitch pattern

29
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.300
to add texture and interest
to the project.

30
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.360
Just like the original.

31
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.780
How many rows to knit?

32
00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:29.300
Hand knitters can measure their
knitting accurately as they go

33
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.100
as we knit on our machines.

34
00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.540
We're stretching that knitting across
the needle bed and hanging weights.

35
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.980
We can't measure accurately
with the knitting hanging on the machine.

36
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:46.420
So how do we know how many rows to knit?
Normally,

37
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.100
swatching gives us that information.

38
00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:55.700
But for a shape like this,
calculating the hypotenuse and the legs

39
00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:01.820
and the number of rows involves math
that is well beyond my capabilities.

40
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.420
With your swatch, you already know your

41
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:10.020
row gauge and you should be able
to estimate how many rows are needed.

42
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:15.600
Keep in mind that scarves and shawls
don't need to be an exact length.

43
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:22.960
The last consideration is the construction
of the knitting on a knitting machine.

44
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.380
In the center section of this design,

45
00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:32.180
we increase along one edge
and decrease along the opposite side.

46
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.940
Shaping in this matter moves your knitting

47
00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:41.160
across the needle bed. So there needs
to be enough room for this movement.

48
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.020
As you knit your scarf or shawl,
you may need to remove the work

49
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:51.640
from the machine with a Garter bar, hand
knitting needle, or just scrap off.

50
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.720
Move the knitting to the other end
of the bed and rehang the stitches.

51
00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.440
And then you can continue the shaping.

52
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.610
With this in mind,

53
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.020
knitting a blanket with this technique
means knitting the blanket in pieces.

54
00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.460
You have to have enough empty
needles to work the center

55
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.740
increase/decrease section.

56
00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:29.940
You could create a very interesting
blanket design with self-striping yarn or

57
00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:35.500
different colors. And alternate
the direction of the strips.

58
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:39.980
You could even turn one strip
with the purl side showing the other

59
00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:44.840
with the knit side showing
for a reversible blanket.

60
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.100
I'm sure this basic technique of knitting

61
00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:52.200
diagonally will inspire projects
in your knitting future.

